WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.742
Secure Go development is a discipline, not
a single package or checklist.

2
00:00:03.822 --> 00:00:07.064
Go gives teams useful advantages: simple
syntax, readable control flow,

3
00:00:07.144 --> 00:00:10.386
a strong standard library, explicit error
returns, garbage collection,

4
00:00:10.466 --> 00:00:11.930
and a service-friendly runtime.

5
00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.520
Those qualities make code easier to review
and maintain,

6
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.064
which can support security work.

7
00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:17.503
The limits matter just as much.

8
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.668
Untrusted input remains untrusted even
when the code is written in Go.

9
00:00:20.748 --> 00:00:22.160
Concurrency can still be wrong.

10
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.593
Resource exhaustion can still affect
availability.

11
00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:26.817
Configuration can still expose risky
behavior.

12
00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:29.355
A clean-looking handler can still return
too much data,

13
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:32.729
ignore an important error, or trust a
value that should have been verified.

14
00:00:32.809 --> 00:00:34.796
Secure Go starts by naming trust
boundaries.

15
00:00:34.876 --> 00:00:37.543
Users, API clients, other services, files,
message queues,

16
00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:41.074
environment variables, databases,
networks, dependencies, build systems,

17
00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:43.611
and operators can all influence
application behavior.

18
00:00:43.691 --> 00:00:46.777
The code should make it clear where data
enters, where it is validated,

19
00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:49.837
where authority is checked, and what
happens when something fails.

20
00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:51.904
Go's style works well with defensive
design.

21
00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:55.383
Small interfaces, concrete types, explicit
conversions, short functions,

22
00:00:55.463 --> 00:00:59.071
predictable errors, and understandable
control flow make assumptions easier to

23
00:00:59.151 --> 00:00:59.569
inspect.

24
00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:02.943
A reviewer should be able to follow the
path from input to decision to output

25
00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:05.376
without hunting through hidden framework
behavior.

26
00:01:05.456 --> 00:01:06.973
The broader habit is consistency.

27
00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:09.563
Code, tests, builds, deployment settings,
monitoring,

28
00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:12.571
and operations should reinforce the same
security expectations.

29
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:14.586
When safe behavior is ordinary and
visible,

30
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:16.809
Go's simplicity becomes a practical
advantage.

31
00:01:16.889 --> 00:01:19.608
Teams should be able to explain not only
what the program does,

32
00:01:19.688 --> 00:01:21.780
but which assumptions keep that behavior
safe.
